Instead, 64-bit wide integers can be used to perform a

packed addition of eight 8-bit values:

uint64_t mask1 = 0x7F7F7F7F7F7F7F7F;

uint64_t mask2 = ~mask1;

uint64_t result_lo = (a & mask1) +

(b & mask1);

uint64_t result_hi = ((a ^ b) & mask2);

uint64_t result = result_lo ^ result_hi;

Note that the packing size is determined purely by the

constant mask that is applied.  A mask of 0x7F7F7F…

treats the data as packed 8 -bit integer, while a mask of

0x7FFF7FFF… treats the data as packed 16 -bit integers.

This a pproach allows for an arbitrary sized data lane, i.e.

one could in theory add packed 9-bit numbers.

